{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EG7Qtzuf1PVQbPGJdjAHjw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and propose tasks for release 05.4 (checksum quartet: md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum).\n\nLooking at the analysis, release 05.4 has 4 use cases, all with specstatus \"spec_complete\" and codestatus \"not started\". I need to read the PRD for checksums to understand the requirements.\n\nBut wait - I don't have the PRD content inline. Let me work with what I know from the context. The constraint says max 1 task, and I should target release 05.4.\n\nFrom the recent git commits, I can see: \"Task 365: Implement checksum quartet cmd/ binaries (prd md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum)\" - so there's already been implementation work on this branch for the checksum quartet.\n\n\nThe code status shows all four use cases as \"not started\" despite the commit history, which is interesting. Since these utilities follow the same pattern for computing and verifying hash digests, I could propose a single consolidated task that covers all four checksums together rather than treating them separately. The commit message tells me task 365 already implemented the binaries for all four checksum utilities, so the remaining work would be tests. Without access to the actual PRD files, I need to infer the requirement IDs from the project structure and propose a single task that covers differential testing for the quartet. cases are:\n- rel05.4-uc001-md5sum\n- rel05.4-uc002-sha1sum  \n- rel05.4-uc003-sha256sum\n- rel05.4-uc004-sha512sum\n\nFor the checksum utilities, the typical PRD requirements would cover:\n- Computing checksums from files and stdin\n- Output format: \"hash  filename\"\n- Check mode (-c flag) to verify checksums\n- Binary/text mode flags (-b, -t)\n- Error handling and exit codes\n\nGiven the constraint of max 1 task, and that task 365 already implemented the binaries, I should propose the test task. But actually, looking at P2: \"Always split implementation and tests into separate tasks.\" So if 365 was implementation, the next task is tests.\n\nBut I'm told not to duplicate existing work. Task 365 exists as a commit, so the code_status might just not be updated yet. I should propose a differential test task for the checksum quartet since that's what would logically follow the implementation. These utilities are nearly identical in structure, differing only in hash algorithm, so a single test task covering all four makes sense.\n\nI need to be careful though—I don't have the actual PRD content. The instructions say all project information is in the project_context field, but the PRDs themselves aren't included, only the analysis and status. I'll work with what I can infer from standard GNU coreutils patterns and reference the PRD files by their expected paths in the project structure. Writing test files for each checksum utility...\n\nI'm realizing I need the actual PRD IDs to properly anchor these tasks, but since I don't have the full PRD documentation, I'll reference the test suite file (test-rel-05.4.yaml) and the use case IDs (rel05.4-uc001 through rel05.4-uc004) as the requirements anchor. The tests should follow the differential testing pattern already established in the codebase, comparing against the GNU coreutils reference binaries. I'm settling on proposing differential tests for the checksum quartet since the implementation commit already exists. Four test files following the same pattern should come to around 200-240 lines total, which fits the task scope. For the PRD references, I'll use the project's naming convention and point to the relevant checksum utility PRDs along with the existing implementation files and testutils package to give the stitch agent everything needed.\n\nDrafting the task specification... I'm realizing I don't actually have the correct PRD numbers for these utilities, so I need to figure out a better approach—either using glob patterns or referencing paths that the stitch agent can reliably find. Looking at the existing PRD naming convention in the project, I can see the pattern, but for batch 5.x utilities the numbers could fall anywhere. Let me step back and reconsider the whole strategy here.\n\nThe stitch agent needs concrete file paths in the required_reading section, so I should focus on what I can predict with certainty: the use case files and test suite files follow a clear naming pattern from the roadmap. I'll reference those as primary reading material and let the stitch agent locate the relevant PRDs by utility name, since the checksum utilities might be covered under a single PRD or separate ones—I'm not certain which.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what comes next. The recent commit implemented all four checksum binaries, but the task description specifically calls for implementing the \"checksum quartet cmd/ binaries\"—which suggests the implementation is already done. Following P2's rule about splitting implementation and tests, the natural next step is a separate test task. I'll anchor the requirements to the test suite spec and use case files, reference the existing implementations in cmd/, point to the testutils package for the DiffTest API, and note where the PRD files should be found.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27595,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27595},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7940aa1-f842-46b9-af8b-e8e30bdf9b3f","uuid":"461be970-e3b9-453f-89c9-fcea84885912"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EG7Qtzuf1PVQbPGJdjAHjw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is a specification-driven experiment that regenerates GNU coreutils/moreutils in Go, verifying parity via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The goal is to stress-test the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation pipeline.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Human writes PRDs/use cases, orchestrator generates Go code in `cmd/` and `pkg/`, differential testing harness (`pkg/testutils`) verifies output parity against GNU binaries.\n\n**Current state:** Releases 00.0 through 05.3 show code status \"not started\" in the analysis (code lives at generation-merged tags). Release 05.4 (checksum quartet: md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum) is spec_complete. The most recent commit on this branch (`1a218ed`) implemented the cmd/ binaries for the checksum quartet (Task 365).\n\n**Current release:** 05.4 — all four use cases (md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum) have specs complete. Implementation was done in Task 365. Differential tests remain.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nTask 365 implemented the checksum quartet binaries. Per P2 (split implementation and tests), the next task is differential tests. All four utilities share identical test structure (differ only in hash algorithm and reference binary name), fitting within a single test task of ~200 lines across 4 files.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Task 366: Differential tests for checksum quartet cmd/ binaries (rel05.4 md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-05.4.yaml (test suite specification with explicit inputs and expected outputs)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel05.4-uc001-md5sum.yaml (md5sum use case)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel05.4-uc002-sha1sum.yaml (sha1sum use case)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel05.4-uc003-sha256sum.yaml (sha256sum use case)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel05.4-uc004-sha512sum.yaml (sha512sum use case)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests, BuildBinary API)\n      - cmd/md5sum/main.go (existing md5sum implementation)\n      - cmd/sha1sum/main.go (existing sha1sum implementation)\n      - cmd/sha256sum/main.go (existing sha256sum implementation)\n      - cmd/sha512sum/main.go (existing sha512sum implementation)\n      - cmd/uniq/uniq_test.go (reference example of differential test file structure in this project)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/md5sum/md5sum_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for md5sum against gmd5sum\n      - path: cmd/sha1sum/sha1sum_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for sha1sum against gsha1sum\n      - path: cmd/sha256sum/sha256sum_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for sha256sum against gsha256sum\n      - path: cmd/sha512sum/sha512sum_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for sha512sum against gsha512sum\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Implement TestDiff in each test file using the project's shared differential\n          testing protocol: testutils.BuildBinary to compile the Go binary, exec.LookPath\n          for the Homebrew reference binary (gmd5sum, gsha1sum, gsha256sum, gsha512sum),\n          t.Skip if reference not found, define []testutils.DiffTest test cases, and call\n          testutils.RunDiffTests.\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Cover stdin hashing (piped input with no file arguments), single file hashing,\n          multiple file hashing, and missing file error behavior. Each scenario is a\n          DiffTest entry with appropriate Args, Stdin, and WorkDir fields. Use temporary\n          files created via t.TempDir for file-based test cases.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Cover check mode (-c flag): create a valid checksum file in the test WorkDir,\n          pass it with -c, and verify the Go binary matches the reference binary output.\n          Include a case with a deliberately wrong checksum to verify failure output parity.\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          Cover binary mode flag (-b) and text mode flag (-t) for file hashing. Verify\n          output format matches the reference binary (two-space separator for text mode,\n          space-asterisk separator for binary mode on GNU). Include --tag format if the\n          PRD specifies it.\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          All four test files follow identical structure, differing only in package name,\n          reference binary name (gmd5sum, gsha1sum, gsha256sum, gsha512sum), and the hash\n          values in check-mode test fixtures. Do not abstract a shared test helper across\n          packages -- each file is self-contained per the project convention.\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          Set LC_ALL=C in the Env field of every DiffTest entry, per the project's locale\n          handling decision (ARCHITECTURE.yaml DD6). This ensures byte-identical output\n          comparison between Go and GNU binaries.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          For check-mode tests, generate the valid checksum file dynamically by running\n          the reference binary on a known input file and capturing its output, then feeding\n          that output as the -c input. This avoids hardcoding hash values that differ per\n          algorithm.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/md5sum/ passes (or skips gracefully if gmd5sum not in PATH)\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/sha1sum/ passes (or skips gracefully if gsha1sum not in PATH)\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/sha256sum/ passes (or skips gracefully if gsha256sum not in PATH)\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/sha512sum/ passes (or skips gracefully if gsha512sum not in PATH)\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: Each test file covers at least 4 DiffTest cases -- stdin, single file, multiple files, and check mode\n      - id: AC6\n        text: No test file imports any package outside testing, os, os/exec, path/filepath, and pkg/testutils\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27595,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27595},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7940aa1-f842-46b9-af8b-e8e30bdf9b3f","uuid":"cc76369c-c8ea-4c2e-b293-b537825c3e31"}
